# Carrier Wound #41: The Unprovisioned Installer Vanished Its Adapter, And A Foreign Adapter Answered For Nodes It Did Not Own.

The D3 burn-in restarted after the wound #38 dream capture, and wound #40 cleared
admission for the support-sweep shape. One gate later — the executor — the
**support-sweep** run `run-live-20260615T021858572Z-ec8eb69c` went terminal at
stepId `inventory-aihero-sources`:

```
run blocked: adapter_unavailable
  "Memory fabric workflow node adapter does not support nodeType
   aihero.support-sweep.source-inventory."
```

Read that error literally: the **memory-fabric** adapter is answering for an
**aihero** node. The wrong subsystem is holding the bag. The planner mounted
aihero, planned an aihero node, and at execution time the memory-fabric adapter
got handed a node it has no business touching — and dutifully reported that it
"does not support" it. That message is true and useless. It points the operator
at memory-fabric to fix an aihero provisioning gap.

## The wound: `return {}` is a silent vanish, not a no-op

Each Cloudflare cartridge installer's `resolve()` returns its dependencies. When
the relay binding is absent, both the memory-fabric and aihero installers took
the same shortcut:

```ts
if (relayBaseUrl === undefined) {
  return {}; // <- the wound: contribute NOTHING
}
```

`combineCloudflareWorkflowCartridgeDependencies` flatMaps the installers' results
and **filters out** any dependency with no `createWorkflowNodeAdapter`. So an
empty `{}` does not "stand down politely" — it **erases the cartridge from the
adapter set entirely**. On the live Worker, memory-fabric WAS provisioned and
aihero was NOT, so the surviving adapter set was `[memory-fabric]` only. With a
single adapter, the combiner returns it directly (no composite), and every node —
including aihero's — routes to memory-fabric, which mis-answers.

This is the **no-hollow-capability rule in adapter form**. A verifier with no
tools reviews nothing — strictly worse than a runaway. An executor with no
adapter blocks nothing it owns — strictly worse than one that fails closed. An
absent adapter is not neutral: it lets a *foreign* adapter answer for nodes it
does not own and aims the operator at the wrong fix. The class is not "aihero
wasn't provisioned." It is "**an unprovisioned cartridge deleted itself from the
routing table instead of staying present to honestly refuse its own work.**"

## The fix: stay present, fail closed, claim your own palette

A shared `createUnprovisionedCartridgeWorkflowNodeAdapter` helper. An unprovisioned
installer no longer vanishes — it contributes an honest fail-closed adapter that:

- For nodeTypes the cartridge **owns** (its config-schema palette): returns a
  **terminal** `secret_denied` blocker naming the cartridge and the missing
  binding — `"workflow/aihero-support-sweep mounted and planned nodeType
  aihero.support-sweep.source-inventory, but its live executor is unprovisioned:
  AIHERO_SUPPORT_SWEEP_RELAY_BASE_URL is not set on the deployed Worker."`
- For nodeTypes it does **not** own: returns `adapter_unavailable`, so the
  composite falls through to the true owner — exactly as a fully-wired adapter's
  own non-matching fall-through behaves.

The owned-node blocker MUST be terminal (`secret_denied`, not
`adapter_unavailable`): `createCompositeWorkflowNodeAdapter` *skips*
`adapter_unavailable` to try the next adapter. An `adapter_unavailable` here would
fall straight through to the foreign adapter and reproduce the misleading error.
`adapter_unavailable` is the **only** code the composite skips; every other
blocker is returned immediately. So ownership routing is enforced by the blocker
code: own it → terminal block at the right owner; disown it → skip to the owner.
Both installers now share this helper — the fix is a **class** fix, not an aihero
instance fix. Memory-fabric unprovisioned blocks `joelclaw.memory.*`; aihero
unprovisioned blocks `aihero.support-sweep.*`; neither vanishes; neither answers
for the other's nodes.

## GRANT, don't loosen — and the second gate is Joel's

This carrier fix makes the failure **honest and precisely routed**. It does NOT
provision the relay. `AIHERO_SUPPORT_SWEEP_RELAY_BASE_URL` is a new network
boundary to customer-private support data — that is an owner decision, not a
carrier patch. Post-fix, the support-sweep run reaches a TRUE blocker that names
the real gap at the right subsystem, instead of a false one pointing at
memory-fabric. The burn-in "both shapes reach captured" cannot complete for the
support-sweep shape until that provisioning gate is decided. The carrier did its
job: it stopped lying about where the wall is.

## The hostile double: drive the real installer assembly, not synthetic adapters

The chaos suite
(`tests/integration/cloudflare-workflow-cartridge-installer.test.ts`) drives the
**real** `workflowCartridgeDependenciesFromWorkerBindings` →
`aiHeroSupportSweepCloudflareCartridgeInstaller` /
`memoryFabricCloudflareCartridgeInstaller`, with bindings parsed through the
production `InstalledWorkflowCartridgeEnvBindingSchema` — the exact resolve()/
combine() path the deployed Worker runs. No polite mock stands in for the
installer.

- **The live wound** (memory provisioned, aihero not): the aihero package is
  PINNED with its source-inventory workflow-node export, reproducing the live
  condition exactly — the planner mounted aihero and planned its node. This makes
  memory-fabric's artifact-backed wrapper PASS export verification and delegate to
  the inner adapter (which emits the skippable `adapter_unavailable`), rather than
  short-circuiting on a `stale_package` blocker that would never reach the aihero
  adapter. Asserts the aihero node reaches a `secret_denied` naming
  `AIHERO_SUPPORT_SWEEP_RELAY_BASE_URL` and `workflow/aihero-support-sweep`, NOT
  "Memory fabric."
- **The class** (both unprovisioned): an aihero node blocks at the aihero binding;
  a `joelclaw.memory.search` node blocks at `MEMORY_RELAY_BASE_URL`; each disowns
  the other's palette. Ownership routing survives the composite regardless of
  registration order.

Falsification proof: reverting the aihero installer to `return {}` turns both
tests RED with `AssertionError: expected 'adapter_unavailable' to be
'secret_denied'` — the chaos suite reproduces the exact live failure code before
the fix. The worker-route shape test
(`cloudflare-worker-route.test.ts`) was the vanish enshrined as a contract
("does not install a workflow-node adapter when the Memory relay is
unconfigured"); it now asserts the anti-vanish contract — the Worker installs an
honest fail-closed adapter even unprovisioned, and still withholds the
provisioning-gated recorders.

## pdf-brain receipt

> "If you don't succeed in saving the data, your database worker has failed, and
> there's no point in hiding this fact. It's better to fail fast, which will cause
> an error that will be logged and (hopefully) noticed and fixed."
> — Saša Jurić, *Elixir in Action, Third Edition*

`return {}` was the database worker swallowing the write failure and returning
`nil` — hiding the fact that it could not do its job. The fix is `File.write!/2`:
fail fast, loudly, at the right owner, so the error is noticed and the real gap
(provisioning) gets fixed instead of being papered over by a foreign adapter's
misleading "does not support." Linked: [[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]],
[[recovery-cant-share-failure-mode]].

## Live outcome

Pending: `pnpm app:deploy` (executor code → full Worker redeploy), then re-submit
the support-sweep run and poll `/runs/:runId/status` to terminal. Expected: a
precise `secret_denied` provisioning blocker naming
`AIHERO_SUPPORT_SWEEP_RELAY_BASE_URL`, routed to aihero — NOT memory-fabric's raw
"does not support" error. This section is appended with the honest live result
once the re-drive is observed — the receipt records what happened on deployed
code, not what the fix predicted.
